# From Parameter Counts to Information Budgets

A large network can occupy a huge parameter space while its selected predictor has a short effective description. Begin with the interactive radius explorer and observe the square-root law directly.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The governing quantity is the additive numerator $R=q+c+K$: quotient complexity, code length, and posterior divergence. The [Kullback–Leibler divergence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kullback%E2%80%93Leibler_divergence) supplies the information-theoretic component, while compression supplies a description-length component.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the certificate theorem and proof sketch</summary>
If $q+c\le B$ and $K\le J$, then addition gives $R=q+c+K\le B+J$. Therefore $B+J\le narepsilon^2$ implies $R\le narepsilon^2$, and for $n>0$ the radius $\sqrt{R/n}$ is at most $arepsilon$. This is the complete deterministic composition argument.
</details>

## Assemble a certificate

Use the laboratory below to compare structural plus posterior cost against the available sample–accuracy budget.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

The next chart displays the three contributions separately. It makes clear which intervention—symmetry reduction, shorter coding, or a better prior—has room to improve the certificate.

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Background: why confidence often costs $\log(1/\delta)$</summary>
Exponential concentration and change-of-measure arguments naturally convert a failure probability $\delta$ into an additive logarithmic penalty. See the overview of [PAC-Bayes bounds](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PAC-learning#PAC-Bayes_bound). In the confidence specialization here, $J\le\log(1/\delta)$ and $B+\log(1/\delta)\le narepsilon^2$ suffice.
</details>

## Let retained architecture, not raw size, set the scale

For active count $a$ and widths $w_1,\ldots,w_L$, use $S=a+\sum_\ell w_\ell$. The next algorithm computes $S$, the total budget, the radius, and the integer sample threshold.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the architecture theorem</summary>
If $q+c\le S$, $K\le\kappa$, and $S+\kappa\le narepsilon^2$, then $R\le S+\kappa\le narepsilon^2$. Hence the requested scale follows. The claim is conditional on an honest bound $q+c\le S$; the architecture formula is not automatically valid for every encoding.
</details>

## Compare competing compressions

A strict reduction $0\le x<y$ implies $\sqrt{x/n}<\sqrt{y/n}$ at every $n>0$. Rank candidate certificates with the comparison routine.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## Widen the coordinates, preserve the idea

Ambient enlargement changes $d$ to $d+k$ and leaves $(q,c,K,n)$ fixed. The resulting radius is horizontal as raw dimension grows.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the explicit overparameterized construction</summary>
For any nonnegative integers $n$ and $e$, choose the profile $(d,q,c,K,n)=(n+e+1,1,0,0,n)$. Then $d>n$ and $R=1$. Whenever $1\le narepsilon^2$, this profile meets the prescribed scale for every positive $\delta$. Because $e$ is arbitrary, ambient surplus is arbitrary while effective complexity remains fixed and nonzero.
</details>

The lesson is precise rather than universal: overparameterization is harmless only when it leaves the certified effective quantities unchanged. The package's article and paper develop the assumptions, algorithms, examples, and limitations in full.

